{% extends 'layouts/page.njk' %}

{% from "copy-button/macro.njk" import copyButton %}
{% from "icon/macro.njk" import icon %}
{% from "status-badge/macro.njk" import statusBadge %}

{# The page about one event, on stock daisyUI: `breadcrumbs` back to the list,
   `card card-border` around each body of evidence, a `timeline` for the
   attempts and `mockup-code` for the message itself. The bespoke grids this
   page used to declare in client/dev-ops.css are gone — the facts list is the
   one place with no component to reach for, and it is plain utilities.

   The two registers it reads in are written as literals here, because Tailwind
   scans this file's text for candidates and never sees a class assembled in
   TypeScript: a label names a fact and never competes with it, and a machine
   string is set in mono because it is a thing to be copied rather than read. #}
{% set factLabel = "pt-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-base-content/50" %}
{% set monoValue = "font-mono text-xs break-all" %}
{% set monoMuted = "font-mono text-xs text-base-content/50" %}

{# One fact: a label, and whatever the caller put under it. A `dl` rather than
   a table, because these are name/value pairs and not a grid of rows. The pair
   is wrapped so it can carry a test id and nothing else — `contents` takes the
   wrapper out of the layout entirely, so every `dt` and `dd` on the page sits
   on the list's own two columns whether its value is one line or four. #}
{% macro fact(label, testId) %}
  <div class="contents" data-testid="{{ testId }}">
    <dt class="{{ factLabel }}">{{ label }}</dt>
    <dd class="min-w-0">{{ caller() }}</dd>
  </div>
{% endmacro %}

{# An instant this page states absolutely and only absolutely: there is no
   column to fit, so the ISO spelling is drawn in full and a date the event
   does not carry is a dash rather than an empty cell nobody can tell from a
   bug. A relative time appears nowhere on this page: it is a figure that
   cannot be quoted, cannot be pasted into a log query and goes stale in an
   open tab.

   The copy button is the point of stating it: what an operator does with a
   timestamp on this page is take it somewhere else. Never beside a dash. #}
{% macro dateFact(label, value, testId) %}
  {% call fact(label, testId) %}
    <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><span class="{{ monoValue if value != "—" else monoMuted }}" data-testid="{{ testId }}-value">{{ value }}</span>{% if value != "—" %}{{ copyButton({ value: value, what: label | lower }) }}{% endif %}</span>
  {% endcall %}
{% endmacro %}

{% block content %}
  {# The way back, first and small: an operator arrives here from a list they
     had filtered, searched and paged, and the whole of that query travels on
     `?from=` so this link puts them back exactly where they were. The id
     beside it is where they are — a trail of one link and no destination is a
     trail that does not say which page it is on. #}
  {# The leaf says which page this is, not what the id is: the id itself heads
     the page two lines below, whole and with the button that copies it. A
     36-character uuid spelled out here pushed the trail onto two lines and
     said it twice, so the crumb keeps the ends — the head an operator
     recognises and the tail they matched it by — and the whole of it stays on
     the title. #}
  {%- set crumbId = (eventId.slice(0, 8) ~ "…" ~ eventId.slice(-4)) if eventId | length > 16 else eventId -%}
  <div class="breadcrumbs text-sm text-base-content/60" data-testid="event-breadcrumbs">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="{{ backHref }}" data-testid="event-back">Events</a></li>
      <li><span class="font-mono" title="{{ eventId }}" data-testid="event-breadcrumb-id">{{ crumbId }}</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {% if banner %}
    {# What the redirect after a write was carrying. A write that redirects has
       to say its own result: the page that lands is a fresh read and knows
       nothing about the click that caused it. #}
    <div role="alert" class="alert alert-soft {{ "alert-success" if banner.role == "success" else ("alert-warning" if banner.role == "warning" else "alert-error") }} mt-3 text-sm" data-testid="event-banner">
      {{ icon({ name: 'exclamation-circle' if banner.role == 'error' else 'exclamation-triangle', class: 'size-4 shrink-0' }) }}
      <span>{{ banner.message }}</span>
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if unavailable %}
    <div role="alert" class="alert alert-error alert-soft mt-3 text-sm" data-testid="event-error">
      {{ icon({ name: 'exclamation-circle', class: 'size-4 shrink-0' }) }}
      <span>This event could not be loaded from GAS.</span>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    {# The header says the three things an operator checks before anything
       else: which id this is, what message it is, and what state it is in.

       The id heads the page, whole: it is the one identifier every event has —
       an audit record publishes no CloudEvent type — and it is what they came
       here to copy, so the copy button sits beside it.

       The type follows underneath when there is one, as the secondary half of
       the identity, with the un-stripped CloudEvent type on its title. The
       status is a soft badge rather than the list's dot: there is no column to
       line up here and one piece of state to say. #}
    <header class="mt-2 mb-4" data-testid="event-header">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-1">
        <h1 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold break-all" data-testid="event-title">{{ eventId }}</h1>{{ copyButton({ value: eventId, what: "event id" }) }}
      </div>
      {% if type %}
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm break-all text-base-content/70" {% if typeTitle %}title="{{ typeTitle }}" {% endif %}data-testid="event-type">{{ type }}</p>
      {% endif %}
      <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
        {{ statusBadge({ status: status, label: statusLabel, role: statusRole, retrying: statusRetrying, variant: "badge" }) }}
        {% if hasFailure or showAttempts %}
          <span class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-base-content/50" title="{{ failureTitle }}" data-testid="event-failure">attempts <span data-testid="event-attempts">{{ attempts }}</span> <span class="text-base-content/30" aria-hidden="true">·</span> <span data-testid="event-last-failure">{{ lastFailureAt }}</span></span>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </header>

    {# The sentence that has to come before the buttons, on the events where it
       is true: this has already been redriven once, by a named person, and it
       failed the same way afterwards. Redriving it again is not wrong — the
       operator may know something the page does not — but it is very unlikely
       to work, and the alternative is the button right beside it.

       A note, not a block. The page states what it knows and leaves the
       decision where it belongs; a disabled Redrive here would be this app
       overruling an operator on the strength of two matching strings. #}
    {% if futileWarning %}
      <div role="note" class="alert alert-warning alert-soft mb-3 text-sm" data-testid="event-futile-warning">
        {{ icon({ name: 'exclamation-triangle', class: 'size-4 shrink-0' }) }}
        <span>{{ futileWarning }}</span>
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# The one decision a dead letter admits. It is a link, not a button that
       does something: `?confirm=redrive` is the same page with a panel on it,
       which back-buttons and reloads like every other state of it, and the
       click that actually writes is the POST inside that panel.

       A panel and not a modal: this state is a url, and a dialog would need
       script the page does not carry to be one. #}
    {% if confirmRedrive %}
      <section class="card card-border mb-4 bg-base-100" data-testid="event-redrive-confirm">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
          <p class="text-sm" data-testid="event-redrive-question">Redrive this event? It will be retried up to 5 times by the poller. This action is audited.</p>
          <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2">
            <form method="post" action="{{ redriveAction }}" data-testid="event-redrive-form">
              <input type="hidden" name="from" value="{{ from }}" data-testid="event-redrive-from">
              <button class="btn btn-sm btn-error" type="submit" data-testid="event-redrive-submit">Redrive</button>
            </form>
            <a class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost" href="{{ cancelHref }}" data-testid="event-redrive-cancel">Cancel</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    {% elif canRedrive %}
      <div class="mb-4 flex items-center gap-2" data-testid="event-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-sm btn-error btn-outline" href="{{ redriveHref }}" data-testid="event-redrive">Redrive</a>
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# The facts, two columns of them: a label and a value, never cut. The list
       is a scanning surface and every string on it is truncated to a column
       width — this page exists because an operator needed the whole of one of
       them. The labels stack above their values on a narrow screen rather than
       squeezing: a value here is an ARN or a traceparent, and neither survives
       a column six characters wide. #}
    {# Two pairs to a line on a wide screen, one on a narrow one. Fifteen facts
       in a single column ran the page's most-read card to a screen and a half
       and left two thirds of every line empty — this is a reference table, and
       a reference table is read across as well as down. The labels stack above
       their values below `sm`, because a value here is an ARN or a traceparent
       and neither survives a column six characters wide. #}
    <section class="card card-border mb-4 bg-base-100" data-testid="event-facts-card">
      <div class="card-body p-4">
        <dl class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-4 gap-y-2.5 text-sm sm:grid-cols-[9.5rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:grid-cols-[9.5rem_minmax(0,1fr)_9.5rem_minmax(0,1fr)]" data-testid="event-facts">
          {# `Queue`, which is what the list's column and the journey table
             below both call it: three surfaces answer the same question about a
             row — which hop it is and where the message was going — and until
             now they asked it in three different words. The test id keeps the
             older name; it is an internal handle, not a label. #}
          {% call fact("Queue", "event-fact-route") %}
            <div data-testid="event-route">{{ routeFrom }} <span class="text-base-content/50">→</span> {% if routeToIsTopic %}<span class="font-mono text-xs" title="{{ routeToTitle }}">{{ routeTo }}</span>{% else %}{{ routeTo }}{% endif %}</div>
            {# The ARN under the sentence it is the machine spelling of. The list
               shows a topic name cut to 26 characters; this is the value an
               operator pastes into the console. #}
            {% if targetRaw %}
              <div class="mt-0.5 flex items-start gap-1"><span class="{{ monoValue }} text-base-content/60" data-testid="event-target-raw">{{ targetRaw }}</span>{{ copyButton({ value: targetRaw, what: "target" }) }}</div>
            {% else %}
              <div class="mt-0.5 {{ monoMuted }}" data-testid="event-route-detail">{{ routeDetail }}</div>
            {% endif %}
          {% endcall %}

          {% call fact("Reference", "event-fact-reference") %}
            {% if segregationRef %}
              <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><a class="link link-hover {{ monoValue }}" href="{{ segregationRefHref }}" title="{{ segregationRefTitle }}" data-testid="event-segregation-ref">{{ segregationRef }}</a>{{ copyButton({ value: segregationRef, what: "reference" }) }}</span>
            {% else %}
              <span class="{{ monoMuted }}" data-testid="event-segregation-ref-none">—</span>
            {% endif %}
          {% endcall %}

          {% call fact("Message id", "event-fact-message-id") %}
            {% if messageId %}
              <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><span class="{{ monoValue }}" data-testid="event-message-id">{{ messageId }}</span>{{ copyButton({ value: messageId, what: "message id" }) }}</span>
            {% else %}
              <span class="{{ monoMuted }}" data-testid="event-message-id-none">—</span>
            {% endif %}
          {% endcall %}

          {# The traceparent is itself the door to the logs: the value opens
             Discover on this trace in a new tab, which is what anybody holding
             a traceparent wants next. When no explorer is configured or the
             event carries no trace id the value stays plain text — readable and
             selectable, just not a door — and no traceparent at all is the em
             dash. #}
          {% call fact("Traceparent", "event-fact-traceparent") %}
            {% if traceparent and traceHref %}
              <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><a class="link link-primary {{ monoValue }}" href="{{ traceHref }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="{{ traceId }}" data-testid="event-traceparent">{{ traceparent }}</a>{{ icon({ name: 'external-link', class: 'size-3 shrink-0 opacity-50' }) }}</span>
            {% elif traceparent %}
              <span class="{{ monoValue }}" data-testid="event-traceparent">{{ traceparent }}</span>
            {% else %}
              <span class="{{ monoMuted }}" data-testid="event-traceparent-none">—</span>
            {% endif %}
          {% endcall %}

          {# Created, stated once and absolutely: the ISO UTC instant an
             operator pastes into a log query, with the button that carries
             it. #}
          {% call fact("Created", "event-fact-created") %}
            <span class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1">
              <span class="{{ monoValue }}" data-testid="event-created-absolute">{{ createdAtAbsolute }}</span>
              {{ copyButton({ value: createdAtAbsolute, what: "created timestamp" }) }}
            </span>
          {% endcall %}

          {{ dateFact("Publication", publicationDate, "event-fact-publication") }}
          {{ dateFact("Completion", completionDate, "event-fact-completion") }}
          {{ dateFact("Last resubmission", lastResubmissionDate, "event-fact-last-resubmission") }}
          {{ dateFact("Claimed at", claimedAt, "event-fact-claimed-at") }}
          {{ dateFact("Claim expires", claimExpiresAt, "event-fact-claim-expires") }}

          {% call fact("Attempts", "event-fact-attempts") %}
            <span class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums" data-testid="event-attempts-value">{{ attempts }}</span>
          {% endcall %}

          {# The last time somebody put this back on the queue, and who. It is the
             fact that turns five identical failures into a story: an event that
             failed five times on its own is a broken message, and one that was
             redriven by a named person and failed the same way afterwards is a
             broken system — which is a different thing to do about it. #}
          {% if lastRedriveFact %}
            {% call fact("Last redrive", "event-fact-last-redrive") %}
              <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><span title="{{ lastRedriveTitle }}" data-testid="event-last-redrive">{{ lastRedriveFact }}</span>{{ copyButton({ value: lastRedriveValue, what: "redrive timestamp" }) }}</span>
            {% endcall %}
          {% endif %}

          {# The reason, whole. The list cuts it to 64 characters and hangs the
             rest off a title; a stack trace summary that has been truncated is
             the single most common reason an operator opens this page at all,
             so it wraps here rather than truncating, and it is the one thing on
             the page allowed the error colour. #}
          {% call fact("Last error", "event-fact-last-error") %}
            {% if errorMessage %}
              <div class="text-xs wrap-anywhere text-error" data-testid="event-error-message"><span class="font-mono font-medium" data-testid="event-error-name">{{ errorName }}</span>: {{ errorMessage }}</div>
              {% if errorAt %}
                <div class="mt-0.5 flex items-center gap-1 {{ monoMuted }}"><span data-testid="event-error-at">at {{ errorAt }}</span>{{ copyButton({ value: errorAt, what: "failure timestamp" }) }}</div>
              {% endif %}
              {# One event's error is almost never one event's problem. This is
                 the click that says whether the operator is looking at an
                 incident or an oddity, and it carries the whole message rather
                 than a prefix of it — the endpoint matches exactly, and a
                 shortened needle would quietly answer a wider question. #}
              {% if errorSearchHref %}
                <div class="mt-1">
                  <a class="link link-primary text-xs" href="{{ errorSearchHref }}" data-testid="event-error-search">Show all events with this error →</a>
                </div>
              {% endif %}
            {% else %}
              <span class="{{ monoMuted }}" data-testid="event-error-none">—</span>
            {% endif %}
          {% endcall %}
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# Every attempt, in the order they happened.

       The page said `attempts 5/5` and then, on one line, why the *last* of
       them failed — the story of the fifth attempt told as if it were the
       story of the event. The four before it are usually where the answer is:
       a timeout, a timeout, then a duplicate key is a different incident from
       four duplicate keys, and neither is visible in a count.

       A timeline, because the order is the content: each rung is the attempt's
       number, when it happened, how long after the one before, and the error
       that ended it — the message wrapping rather than truncating, because a
       wrapped stack summary is the whole reason an operator opened this page.
       Compact, so the spine sits at the left margin and the evidence gets the
       width. #}
    <section class="card card-border mb-4 bg-base-100" data-testid="event-attempts-card">
      <div class="card-body p-4">
        <h2 class="card-title text-sm" data-testid="event-attempts-heading">Attempts</h2>
        {% if attemptHistory | length %}
          <ul class="timeline timeline-vertical timeline-compact -ms-2" data-testid="event-attempt-list">
            {% for attempt in attemptHistory %}
              <li data-testid="event-attempt">
                {% if not loop.first %}<hr>{% endif %}
                <div class="timeline-middle">{{ icon({ name: 'circle-x', class: 'size-3.5 text-error' }) }}</div>
                <div class="timeline-end mb-2 pt-0.5">
                  <div class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums">
                    <span class="font-medium text-base-content/70" data-testid="event-attempt-number">{{ attempt.number }}</span>
                    <span class="text-base-content/30" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                    {# The instant, then the gap in front of it. `4h 24m ago`
                       was the first thing on this line and it said nothing:
                       five attempts inside one minute all round to one phrase,
                       which is exactly the case this list is read in. The
                       instant is whole, milliseconds and all, and the delta
                       beside it is the arithmetic nobody should have to do —
                       four attempts at `+273ms`, `+512ms`, `+1.1s` is a
                       service retrying into a wall, and the shape of that is
                       visible at a glance or not at all. #}
                    <span title="{{ attempt.title }}" data-testid="event-attempt-when">{{ attempt.absolute }}</span>
                    {% if attempt.delta %}
                      <span class="text-base-content/30" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                      <span class="text-base-content/50" data-testid="event-attempt-delta">{{ attempt.delta }}</span>
                    {% endif %}
                  </div>
                  <div class="font-mono text-xs wrap-anywhere text-error/80" data-testid="event-attempt-error"><span class="font-medium" data-testid="event-attempt-name">{{ attempt.name }}</span>: {{ attempt.message }}</div>
                </div>
                {% if not loop.last or attemptOutcome %}<hr>{% endif %}
              </li>
            {% endfor %}
            {# Where the story ends. A list of five failures that simply stops
               reads as an event still failing; this is the rung that says which
               way it actually went. #}
            {% if attemptOutcome %}
              <li>
                <hr>
                <div class="timeline-middle">{{ icon({ name: 'octagon-alert', class: 'size-3.5 text-base-content/40' }) }}</div>
                <div class="timeline-end pt-0.5 text-xs text-base-content/60" data-testid="event-attempt-outcome">{{ attemptOutcome }}</div>
              </li>
            {% endif %}
          </ul>
        {% else %}
          {# One line, under the heading it belongs to. A centred sentence in
             six rems of white space made the absence of a thing look like a
             section of its own — and this absence is the ordinary case for
             every event written before attempt history existed. #}
          <p class="text-sm text-base-content/55" data-testid="event-attempts-empty">No attempt details recorded (event predates attempt history).</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </section>

    {# Every row the endpoint holds under this event id, in the order it
       returned them: the inbox row that received it, the outbox row that
       published it, the retry that followed. The question "where else did this
       message go?" is the one an operator asks next, and before this it was a
       search they had to run themselves.

       The row they are already on is marked rather than unlinked: a table
       where one row has no link reads as a broken row.

       Above the payload, because it is four lines about where this message
       went and the payload is a screenful of the message itself: a two-row
       table pushed below a hundred lines of JSON is a table nobody scrolls
       to. #}
    <section class="card card-border mb-4 bg-base-100" data-testid="event-journey-card">
      <div class="card-body p-4">
        <h2 class="card-title text-sm" data-testid="event-journey-heading">Journey — every hop with this event id</h2>
        {% if journey | length %}
          <div class="max-w-full overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="table table-sm" data-testid="event-journey">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Queue</th>
                  <th>Status</th>
                  {# How long each hop itself took. The table said when every hop
                     started and never how long any of them ran, so the one
                     question it is actually read for — which hop is slow — was
                     the one it could not answer. #}
                  <th class="text-right">Took</th>
                  <th class="text-right">Created</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for hop in journey %}
                  <tr class="{{ "bg-base-200" if hop.isCurrent else "hover:bg-base-200" }}" data-testid="event-journey-row">
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                      <a class="link link-hover" href="{{ hop.href }}" data-testid="event-journey-link">{{ hop.source }}</a>
                      {% if hop.isCurrent %}<span class="badge badge-ghost badge-xs ms-1" data-testid="event-journey-current">this event</span>{% endif %}
                    </td>
                    <td>{{ statusBadge({ status: hop.status, label: hop.statusLabel, role: hop.statusRole, retrying: hop.statusRetrying }) }}</td>
                    <td class="text-right font-mono text-xs tabular-nums whitespace-nowrap text-base-content/60" title="Time from created to completed on this hop" data-testid="event-journey-took">{{ hop.took }}</td>
                    <td class="text-right font-mono text-xs tabular-nums whitespace-nowrap" title="{{ hop.createdAtTitle }}" data-testid="event-journey-created">{{ hop.createdAt }}</td>
                  </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        {% else %}
          <p class="py-6 text-center text-sm text-base-content/55" data-testid="event-journey-empty">No other hops could be read for this event id.</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </section>
    {# The payload, in full and as text. It is the whole reason a single-event
       page exists: everything above is plumbing, and this is the message.

       A plain bordered block on the page's own surface. `mockup-code` drew it
       as a screenshot of a terminal — three coloured traffic lights, a dark
       window in a light page — which is decoration pretending to be a frame
       around the one piece of evidence on this page. The line numbers are the
       part that earned its keep, so they stay — drawn by the stylesheet from
       a `data-line` attribute rather than written into the markup, so a
       selection dragged across the block copies the payload and not a numbered
       listing of it.

       Rendered through nunjucks' own escaping, like every other value here: a
       payload carrying `<script>` is a payload an operator can read, and never
       something this page runs. #}
    <section class="card card-border bg-base-100" data-testid="event-payload-card">
      <div class="card-body p-4">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <h2 class="card-title text-sm" data-testid="event-payload-heading">Payload</h2>
          {% if payloadJson %}{{ copyButton({ value: payloadJson, what: "payload" }) }}{% endif %}
        </div>
        {% if payloadJson %}
          <div class="max-h-96 w-full overflow-auto rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 py-2 font-mono text-xs" data-testid="event-payload">
            {% for line in payloadJson.split("\n") %}<pre class="flex gap-3 px-3" data-testid="event-payload-line"><span class="w-8 shrink-0 text-right tabular-nums text-base-content/30 before:content-[attr(data-line)]" data-line="{{ loop.index }}" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>{{ line }}</code></pre>{% endfor %}
          </div>
        {% else %}
          <p class="py-6 text-center text-sm text-base-content/55" data-testid="event-payload-empty">This event carries no payload.</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </section>

  {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
